{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Scan changed files for secrets on stop",
  "description": "When the agent stops, scan the files changed in the working tree for hardcoded secrets or API keys and report any findings. Read-only: reports, never edits or commits.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "agentStop"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "The agent has stopped. Scan only the files that are changed in the working tree for hardcoded secrets or API keys. Steps: (1) run `git status --short` and `git diff --name-only HEAD` to get the list of changed/untracked files; (2) for those files, search their contents for likely secrets - e.g. AWS access keys (AKIA/ASIA followed by 16 chars), AWS secret keys, Google API keys (AIza...), GitHub tokens (ghp_/gho_/ghs_/github_pat_), Slack tokens (xox...), Stripe keys (sk_live_/rk_live_), private key blocks (-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----), JWTs, generic high-entropy assignments to names like password/passwd/secret/token/api_key/apikey/access_key, and connection strings embedding credentials; (3) report each finding as file path, line number, and the matched KEY NAME or token type only - do NOT echo the full secret value, mask it (show at most the first 4 characters). Distinguish real secrets from obvious test placeholders (e.g. the project uses tool=claimledger and email=hackathon@example.com as PubMed identifiers, which are NOT secrets). If nothing is found, say so. This hook is read-only: do not edit, delete, or commit any file; only report. If a real secret is found, recommend the user rotate it and move it to an environment variable or ignored config, but take no action yourself."
  }
}
